docs/v1.3.0/PA4_COMPARATIVE_EVALUATION.md:21:The input provider config, dataset digest, task objective, budgets, model, context/output limits, and sampling are common to all arms. Each run receives a fresh isolated workspace and HOME. Arm order rotates by task and replicate. Failed and interrupted runs retain their logs and are included in the denominator. The evaluator verifies the same deterministic artifact and regression criteria after each arm.
docs/v1.3.0/PA4_COMPARATIVE_EVALUATION.md:23:The score reports deterministic task success first, then end-to-end `eval/run.mjs` process wall time and inference request counts. Wall time includes preparation and deterministic post-run evaluation, and failed invocations remain in the wall-time and success-rate denominators. Efficiency comparison requires at least one serial success, ten attempts per arm, no lower observed success, and a no-worse 95% Wilson lower bound. Independent acceptance is not collected by this development scorer and remains a separate review gate. Token fields retain runner provenance; estimates remain estimates, and absent telemetry remains unavailable. A comparison does not establish improvement from latency or throughput alone. The batch reviewer role is not treated as an independent acceptance verdict.
docs/v1.3.0/PA4_COMPARATIVE_EVALUATION.md:27:Local deterministic tests cover arm mapping, operation-spec graph construction, Unicode/objective retention, sidecar terminal-receipt handling, scorer denominators, and zero-acceptance handling. No inference comparison was run while the repository's GATEWAY claim was held by another workstream. The harness refuses `qualification: true` and rejects T3/T4 datasets until their interaction semantics are preserved across all arms: this implementation is a repeatable T1/T2 development protocol, not held-out evidence. It does not claim measured comparative outcomes, security qualification, or acceptance superiority. A separately authorized held-out run and independent review remain required.
